  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how hydrothermal vent plumes shape microbial and viral activity, especially around iron, sulfur, and carbon cycling. A buoyant vent plume rises from the seafloor and carries microorganisms along with reduced chemicals and minerals into the water column, where some material is exported to other vent fields and the broader ocean. Insets suggest that iron and manganese are transformed into mineral particles, while siderophores released by microbes bind iron and help keep it available.\n\nWithin the plume, a food web links chemoautotrophs, protists, zooplankton, heterotrophic bacteria, viruses, dissolved organic matter, and dissolved inorganic carbon. Chemoautotrophs appear to use reduced sulfur compounds and carbon dioxide, forming biomass that supports higher trophic levels and feeds back into organic matter pools.\n\nThe right side emphasizes the role of viruses. Viral infection and replication introduce DNA, RNA, and proteins into host cells, and viral lysis returns cellular material to the environment. Another inset indicates that viral auxiliary metabolic genes may enhance sulfur oxidation, and a final panel suggests that lysis under these conditions could reduce elemental sulfur storage. Overall, the figure portrays vent plumes as hotspots where microbial metabolism, iron acquisition, and viruses interact to control biogeochemical fluxes.",
